<DocsLayout
  title="CAD geometry"
  subtitle="Drawing entities, constrained sketches, parametric histories, meshes, and exact solids in a shared geometry core."
  description="CADCAMFUN CAD geometry: entity schemas, sketches, parametric replay, boundary representation, STEP exchange, and drawings."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="drawing" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Drawing entities</h2>
<p><code>@cadcamfun/core</code> exports CAD functions both directly and under the <code>cad</code> namespace. Schema-first 2D entities use an <code>_tag</code> discriminator: Line, Circle, Arc, Polyline, Rectangle, Ellipse, Spline, Text, Dimension, Hatch, and Block. Each entity has a stable identifier, layer, visibility, and lock state; selection belongs to editor state.</p>
    <p>Entity geometry uses millimetres and radians. Arc angles are normalized to a full turn, and equal start/end angles represent a full circle. A local entity transform applies scale, rotation, then translation; <code>Affine2D</code> supports composed transforms that can include shear. The scene-element and parametric-history adapters have their own documented angular fields, including degree-valued placement and feature controls.</p>
    <p><code>entitySchemaOf(tag)</code> returns the schema for one entity kind. Decode imported data before calling geometry functions. Bounds, tessellation, intersections, offsets, and snapping are separate operations; rendering a tessellated curve does not replace its analytic entity.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="sketch" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Constrained sketches</h2>
<p>The sketch model contains named points, lines between points, circles with a center and radius, and counter-clockwise arcs. Shared point identifiers express shared endpoints. Constraints include coincidence, horizontal and vertical alignment, distances, lengths, parallel and perpendicular lines, equal lengths and radii, tangency, symmetry, and geometric incidence.</p>
    <p>The numerical solver minimizes constraint residuals with a Levenberg–Marquardt iteration and a central-difference Jacobian. It reports degrees of freedom, free points, redundant equations, and conflicting constraints. A result can be fully constrained, under-constrained, over-constrained, conflicting, or failed. An under-constrained solution can satisfy every dimension while still allowing motion; callers must preserve that distinction.</p>
    <p>Sketch constraints receive numbers. Parametric histories resolve parameter expressions before invoking the solver. Sketch angle constraints are radians; use the feature's own input convention when converting a user-entered dimension.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="history" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Parametric histories</h2>
<p>A parametric part stores its parameters and ordered feature history. Rebuilding evaluates expressions and features in sequence so a feature can reference sketches, planes, bodies, or tools created earlier. The core includes extrusions, revolutions, lofts and sweeps, booleans, holes, patterns, face edits, text, threads, sheet-metal features, and mating controls. Availability of an exact representation depends on the particular feature sequence.</p>
    <p><code>rebuild</code> produces mesh bodies and feature issues. <code>rebuildExact</code> replays the history through the boundary-representation kernel and records why a body lacks an exact representation. Keep the history as the editable source; generated bodies are derived output. A failed or unsupported feature must remain visible through its issue record.</p>
    <DocCallout title="Read the replay result">
      <p>Mesh output can exist when exact replay cannot represent a feature. Check the body and issue records before making claims about exact geometry, volume, or export fidelity.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="mesh" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Mesh geometry and placement</h2>
<p>Meshes use indexed triangle geometry. The CAD layer provides primitive construction, mesh booleans, sweep and loft helpers, topology and normal utilities, and geometry cleanup. The CAM model adapter converts supported scene elements to meshes, including nested components, so analysis and toolpath generation can use the same placed geometry.</p>
    <p>Element position, rotation, scale, and translation affect the resulting part. Exact scene placement mirrors the viewport transform about the bounding-box center. Non-uniform scaling can remove an element's exact-model path; scene conversion reports a reason and may use a faceted representation during export.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="brep" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Exact boundary representation</h2>
<p><code>cad.brep</code> exposes analytic and NURBS geometry with face, loop, edge-use, edge, and vertex topology. Its modules cover primitives, sweeps, lofts, booleans, face edits, blends, sewing, tessellation, classification, validation, mass properties, and STEP reading and writing. <code>history-brep.ts</code> and <code>scene-step.ts</code> connect this kernel to parametric parts and placed scene elements.</p>
    <p><code>POST /api/cad/exact/analyze</code> returns per-part validity issues, volume, area, centroid, bounds, topology counts, surface counts, recognized holes, and machining classification. It also returns skipped elements with reasons and aggregate totals. A prismatic machining result reports outlines, holes, pockets, and top/bottom levels; unsupported parts carry a reason.</p>
    <p><code>POST /api/cad/exact/assembly</code> reports interference, expected overlaps, touching and undetermined pairs, bill-of-material items, joints, and fastener plans. Undetermined geometry is a reported outcome, not proof of clearance. These analysis routes compute results without altering the drawing.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="exchange" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Import and export</h2>
<p>The core has DXF and drawing-import helpers, a prismatic STEP interpretation path, and an exact STEP B-rep reader. The prismatic path reads a footprint and extrusion height for suitable plates, brackets, and flanges; it reports features that a prism cannot describe. Choose the exact reader when exact supported STEP topology is needed, and inspect diagnostics from either path.</p>
    <p><code>sceneToStep</code> writes a scene as STEP AP214. Exact supported solids retain exact geometry; mesh-only solids can be emitted as faceted solids. Non-solid elements are skipped with reasons. The <code>faceted</code> option controls that fallback. A response without <code>step</code> contains no exportable solid.</p>
    <p>The HTTP exact routes accept either <code>elements</code> or <code>drawingId</code>; explicit elements take precedence. Snapshot arrays allow 1–2,000 elements and an optional selection of up to 200 identifiers. With a stored drawing, the sidecar reads the scene under drawing access rules. A missing source, empty drawing, unknown drawing, or denied drawing produces the corresponding request or access error.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="sheets" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Technical drawings</h2>
<p>The <code>drawing</code> namespace provides projections, sections, dimensions, sheet composition, and SVG output from 3D models. This is a separate representation from the editable 2D entity drawing and from a machining program. Projection and section geometry should be regenerated after model changes; a sheet export captures the model state used to build it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="modules" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Where to extend geometry</h2>
<p>Start with <code>packages/core/src/cad/index.ts</code> for the public CAD surface. Entity operations live beside <code>entities.ts</code>; constraint solving is in <code>sketch.ts</code>; mesh history is in <code>history.ts</code>; exact replay is in <code>history-brep.ts</code>; exact topology and exchange are under <code>brep/</code>. <code>packages/core/src/drawing/</code> owns sheet output. Supporting modules in the same folder include <code>expr.ts</code> (parameter expressions such as <code>width / 2 + 3</code>, resolved in dependency order with cycles reported as errors), <code>tolerance.ts</code> (chord-sagitta segment counts for curves), <code>gear.ts</code> (ISO 53 involute spur-gear outlines), <code>thread.ts</code>, <code>sheet-metal.ts</code>, <code>fasteners.ts</code>, <code>recipes.ts</code> (generators that emit complete, fully constrained feature histories), <code>dfm.ts</code> (design-for-manufacture findings with measured and required values), and <code>dxf.ts</code>.</p>
    <p>Geometry functions operate on values without opening application storage. Sidecar services handle access and persistence, and desktop adapters handle interaction and rendering. Keep conversions explicit at the boundaries so a display mesh, an exact solid, and a saved history retain their distinct meaning.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="exact-example" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Build, validate, and export a solid</h2>
<p>This complete core example constructs a 40 × 25 × 8 mm box with one corner at the world origin, checks its topology, measures it, and exports STEP text. It requires only the workspace core package and performs no application writes.</p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="An exact box and its STEP text">
{String.raw`import { cad } from "@cadcamfun/core"

const solid = cad.brep.makeBox(40, 25, 8)
const report = cad.brep.validateBrep(solid)
if (!report.valid) throw new Error(JSON.stringify(report.issues))

const mass = cad.brep.massProperties(solid)
console.log(mass.volume.toFixed(3)) // "8000.000" cubic millimetres
console.log(mass.centroid) // approximately { x: 20, y: 12.5, z: 4 }
const step = cad.brep.writeStepBreps([{ name: "Bracket blank", brep: solid }], {
  author: "Workshop",
  timestamp: "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z"
})
console.log(step.startsWith("ISO-10303-21;")) // true`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The explicit timestamp makes repeated exchange output reproducible. Without it, STEP writing uses the current time. <code>massProperties</code> also returns area, per-face areas, and an inertia tensor about the centroid at unit density; volume is mm³ and area is mm². Convert density explicitly when calculating a physical mass.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>packages/core/src/cad/brep/primitives.ts</code>, <code>mass.ts</code>, <code>validate.ts</code>, and <code>step.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="validation-options" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Validation and conversion options</h2>
<p><code>validateBrep</code> returns <code>valid</code>, issue records, <code>manifoldSolid</code>, and optional genus/volume. <code>manifoldSolid</code> means closed shells with every edge used twice in opposite senses. The default geometric distance tolerance scales as 10⁻⁵ of model size with a 10⁻⁶ mm floor. A caller can supply <code>tol</code>, skip volume/orientation checking with <code>skipVolume</code>, or use a coarse tessellation for orientation with <code>coarseVolume</code>.</p>
    <p>The exact-analysis API's <code>machining</code> flag defaults to true. STEP scene export's <code>faceted</code> fallback defaults to true, and assembly fastener planning's <code>washers</code> option defaults to true. Selection IDs refer to scene elements, including component children resolved by the scene traversal. These HTTP options affect analysis/export, not the stored drawing.</p>
    <p>Scene export returns per-part <code>exact</code>, <code>faces</code>, <code>valid</code>, and an optional note. Check those fields alongside <code>skipped</code>: a valid faceted solid can be exportable without retaining analytic surfaces.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>packages/core/src/cad/brep/validate.ts</code>, <code>scene-analysis.ts</code>, <code>scene-step.ts</code>, <code>assembly-analysis.ts</code>, and <code>packages/api/src/cad-exact.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Repair a failed rebuild or export</h2>
<table>
      <thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Next inspection</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sketch remains under-constrained</td><td>Read degrees of freedom and free-point IDs. Add the missing position/orientation constraints; a dimensioned line with only one fixed endpoint can still rotate.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sketch constraints conflict</td><td>Use named conflict IDs and the residual to locate incompatible dimensions. Repeating a compatible equation is redundancy; two different required lengths are a conflict.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>History feature references an unknown body or plane</td><td>Check feature order and identifiers. Dependencies must exist earlier in the replay, and suppression/deletion can invalidate later references.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exact model unavailable after scaling</td><td>Inspect non-uniform scale and the element's reported reason. Use supported uniform placement or accept and label a faceted export.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>STEP result has skipped items or no text</td><td>Check whether the selection contains solids and whether faceted fallback is enabled. Empty or 2D-only selections cannot produce a solid STEP file.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Primitive dimensions must be positive and finite. Direct geometry functions can throw range errors for invalid dimensions; transport and service layers must convert those failures into their own typed results. Rebuild issues are keyed to feature IDs so a failure can be repaired at its source rather than concealed by retaining an earlier mesh.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>packages/core/src/cad/sketch.test.ts</code>, <code>history.ts</code>, <code>scene-step.ts</code>, and <code>apps/sidecar/test/cad-exact.test.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

    <section id="sketch-walkthrough" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Solve a sketch step by step</h2>
    <p>A sketch is plain data: an array of <code>entities</code> (points, lines, circles, arcs) and an array of <code>constraints</code>. Lines, circles, and arcs refer to points by identifier, so two lines that share an endpoint share the same point ID. The solver moves only point coordinates and circle radii; it never adds or removes entities.</p>
    <figure class="not-prose my-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 520 260" role="img" aria-label="Constrained rectangle sketch with points p1 to p4, lines l1 to l4, fixed origin, horizontal and vertical constraints, and length dimensions 40 and 25" style="width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto">
        <rect x="0" y="0" width="520" height="260" fill="none"/>
        <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none">
          <line x1="80" y1="200" x2="400" y2="200"/><line x1="400" y1="200" x2="400" y2="40"/>
          <line x1="400" y1="40" x2="80" y2="40"/><line x1="80" y1="40" x2="80" y2="200"/>
        </g>
        <g fill="currentColor" font-size="13" font-family="monospace">
          <circle cx="80" cy="200" r="5"/><circle cx="400" cy="200" r="4"/><circle cx="400" cy="40" r="4"/><circle cx="80" cy="40" r="4"/>
          <text x="52" y="222">p1 (fixed)</text><text x="408" y="222">p2</text><text x="408" y="36">p3</text><text x="58" y="34">p4</text>
          <text x="210" y="194">l1 · horizontal</text><text x="410" y="125">l2 · vertical</text>
          <text x="210" y="56">l3 · horizontal</text><text x="6" y="125">l4 · vertical</text>
        </g>
        <g stroke="#2f80ed" fill="#2f80ed" font-size="13" font-family="monospace">
          <line x1="80" y1="235" x2="400" y2="235"/><text x="215" y="252" stroke="none">width = 40</text>
          <line x1="470" y1="200" x2="470" y2="40"/><text x="440" y="125" stroke="none" transform="rotate(-90 478 125)">depth = 25</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="text-[13px]">The sketch used in the examples: four points, four lines, one fixed point, four orientation constraints, and two length dimensions. Every degree of freedom is removed, so the solver reports <code>fully-constrained</code> with <code>dof = 0</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Solve a fully constrained rectangle">
{"import { cad } from \u0022@cadcamfun/core\u0022\n\nconst sketch = {\n  entities: [\n    { id: \u0022p1\u0022, kind: \u0022point\u0022, x: 0, y: 0 },\n    { id: \u0022p2\u0022, kind: \u0022point\u0022, x: 38, y: 1 },   // rough positions are fine:\n    { id: \u0022p3\u0022, kind: \u0022point\u0022, x: 39, y: 26 },  // the solver moves them\n    { id: \u0022p4\u0022, kind: \u0022point\u0022, x: 1, y: 24 },\n    { id: \u0022l1\u0022, kind: \u0022line\u0022, p1: \u0022p1\u0022, p2: \u0022p2\u0022 },\n    { id: \u0022l2\u0022, kind: \u0022line\u0022, p1: \u0022p2\u0022, p2: \u0022p3\u0022 },\n    { id: \u0022l3\u0022, kind: \u0022line\u0022, p1: \u0022p3\u0022, p2: \u0022p4\u0022 },\n    { id: \u0022l4\u0022, kind: \u0022line\u0022, p1: \u0022p4\u0022, p2: \u0022p1\u0022 },\n  ],\n  constraints: [\n    { type: \u0022fixed\u0022, point: \u0022p1\u0022, x: 0, y: 0 },\n    { type: \u0022horizontal\u0022, line: \u0022l1\u0022 },\n    { type: \u0022horizontal\u0022, line: \u0022l3\u0022 },\n    { type: \u0022vertical\u0022, line: \u0022l2\u0022 },\n    { type: \u0022vertical\u0022, line: \u0022l4\u0022 },\n    { id: \u0022width\u0022, type: \u0022length\u0022, line: \u0022l1\u0022, value: 40 },\n    { id: \u0022depth\u0022, type: \u0022length\u0022, line: \u0022l2\u0022, value: 25 },\n  ],\n}\n\nconst result = cad.solveSketch(sketch)\nresult.status     // \u0022fully-constrained\u0022\nresult.dof        // 0\nresult.iterations // 3\nresult.residual   // < 1e-9\n// points now at (0,0) (40,0) (40,25) (0,25)"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Run the same sketch without the <code>fixed</code> constraint and the result changes to <code>under-constrained</code> with <code>dof = 2</code> and <code>free = ["p1", "p2", "p3", "p4"]</code>. The rectangle still has the right size, but it can slide in X and Y. Add a third length, <code>&#123; id: "w2", type: "length", line: "l3", value: 50 &#125;</code>, and the status becomes <code>conflicting</code>. <code>conflicts</code> then lists the named constraints that cannot be satisfied together, worst first (for example <code>w2</code> and <code>width</code>). Name the constraints you care about with <code>id</code>; unnamed constraints are reported with a generated label such as <code>vertical#4</code>.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>maxIterations</code></td><td>200</td><td>Upper bound on Levenberg–Marquardt iterations before the solve is reported as <code>failed</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tolerance</code></td><td>1e-9</td><td>The largest absolute residual accepted as solved.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Result field</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>sketch</code></td><td>The input sketch with solved coordinates and radii.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td><code>fully-constrained</code>, <code>under-constrained</code>, <code>over-constrained</code>, <code>conflicting</code>, or <code>failed</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dof</code></td><td>Remaining degrees of freedom.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>free</code></td><td>Point IDs that can still move, and circle IDs whose radius can still change.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>conflicts</code></td><td>Constraint IDs that could not be satisfied, worst first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>redundant</code></td><td>Number of redundant equations (rank deficiency).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>iterations</code>, <code>residual</code>, <code>message</code></td><td>Solver diagnostics.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Malformed input throws">
      <p>A sketch that refers to an unknown point or entity is malformed rather than unsolvable, and <code>solveSketch</code> throws a <code>SketchError</code>. Validate imported sketches before solving them in a loop.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="constraint-reference" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Constraint reference</h2>
    <p>Every constraint is an object with a <code>type</code> and an optional <code>id</code>. Lengths are millimetres and angles are radians. In a parametric history, any <code>value</code> may also be a parameter expression string.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Fields</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>fixed</code></td><td><code>point</code>, optional <code>x</code>, <code>y</code></td><td>Pins a point, optionally to explicit coordinates.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>coincident</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code></td><td>Two points occupy the same position.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>horizontal</code> / <code>vertical</code></td><td><code>line</code></td><td>A line is parallel to X / Y.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>horizontalPoints</code> / <code>verticalPoints</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code></td><td>Two points share a Y / X coordinate.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>distance</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>value</code></td><td>Euclidean distance between two points.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>distanceX</code> / <code>distanceY</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>value</code></td><td>Signed horizontal / vertical offset.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>length</code></td><td><code>line</code>, <code>value</code></td><td>Length of a line.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>parallel</code> / <code>perpendicular</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code></td><td>Relationship between two lines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>equalLength</code> / <code>equalRadius</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code></td><td>Equal line lengths / circle or arc radii.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>radius</code> / <code>diameter</code></td><td><code>circle</code>, <code>value</code></td><td>Size of a circle or arc.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>angle</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>value</code></td><td>Counter-clockwise angle from line <code>a</code> to line <code>b</code>, in radians.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pointOnLine</code> / <code>pointOnCircle</code></td><td><code>point</code> and <code>line</code> / <code>circle</code></td><td>Incidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>midpoint</code></td><td><code>point</code>, <code>line</code></td><td>A point sits at the middle of a line.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>symmetric</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>line</code></td><td>Two points mirror each other about a line.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tangentLineCircle</code></td><td><code>line</code>, <code>circle</code></td><td>A line touches a circle or arc.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tangentCircles</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, optional <code>internal</code></td><td>Circles touch externally (distance = r1 + r2) or internally (distance = |r1 − r2|).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>concentric</code></td><td><code>a</code>, <code>b</code></td><td>Shared centre of circles or arcs.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Arcs are counter-clockwise from <code>start</code> to <code>end</code> about <code>center</code>. A circle's radius is a solver variable, so a circle without a <code>radius</code>, <code>diameter</code>, <code>equalRadius</code>, or tangency constraint appears in <code>free</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="history-walkthrough" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Build a parametric part</h2>
    <p>A <code>History</code> is a parameter table plus an ordered feature list. Parameters are numbers or expressions of other parameters. They are resolved in dependency order, and a cycle is an error that names the loop. Features refer to parameters by name in any dimension field.</p>
    <DocViewer3D title="Parametric plate: 40 × 25 × 8 mm with a Ø5 through hole" caption="Result of the history example below: the sketch is solved, extruded by thick = 8 mm, and the hole (diameter = width / 8 = 5 mm) is subtracted. Exact replay of this history has a volume of 7,842.92 mm³ (8,000 − π·2.5²·8)." solids={[{"kind": "extrude", "profile": [[0, 0], [40, 0], [40, 25], [0, 25]], "holes": [[[22.5, 12.5], [22.479, 12.826], [22.415, 13.147], [22.31, 13.457], [22.165, 13.75], [21.983, 14.022], [21.768, 14.268], [21.522, 14.483], [21.25, 14.665], [20.957, 14.81], [20.647, 14.915], [20.326, 14.979], [20.0, 15.0], [19.674, 14.979], [19.353, 14.915], [19.043, 14.81], [18.75, 14.665], [18.478, 14.483], [18.232, 14.268], [18.017, 14.022], [17.835, 13.75], [17.69, 13.457], [17.585, 13.147], [17.521, 12.826], [17.5, 12.5], [17.521, 12.174], [17.585, 11.853], [17.69, 11.543], [17.835, 11.25], [18.017, 10.978], [18.232, 10.732], [18.478, 10.517], [18.75, 10.335], [19.043, 10.19], [19.353, 10.085], [19.674, 10.021], [20.0, 10.0], [20.326, 10.021], [20.647, 10.085], [20.957, 10.19], [21.25, 10.335], [21.522, 10.517], [21.768, 10.732], [21.983, 10.978], [22.165, 11.25], [22.31, 11.543], [22.415, 11.853], [22.479, 12.174]]], "height": 8}]} />
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Plate with a parametric hole">
{"import { cad } from \u0022@cadcamfun/core\u0022\n\nconst history = {\n  parameters: { width: 40, depth: 25, thick: 8, hole: \u0022width / 8\u0022 },\n  features: [\n    // the rectangle sketch from the previous section, with dimension\n    // values replaced by parameter names: value: \u0022width\u0022, value: \u0022depth\u0022\n    { id: \u0022s1\u0022, type: \u0022sketch\u0022, plane: \u0022XY\u0022, sketch: rectangleSketch },\n    { id: \u0022e1\u0022, type: \u0022extrude\u0022, sketch: \u0022s1\u0022, depth: \u0022thick\u0022, operation: \u0022new\u0022 },\n    { id: \u0022h1\u0022, type: \u0022hole\u0022, plane: \u0022XY\u0022, offset: \u0022thick\u0022,\n      at: [20, 12.5], diameter: \u0022hole\u0022, depth: \u0022thick\u0022 },\n  ],\n}\n\nconst mesh = cad.rebuild(history)\nmesh.parameters // { width: 40, depth: 25, thick: 8, hole: 5 }\nmesh.bodies     // one mesh body, id \u0022e1\u0022\nmesh.issues     // []\n\nconst exact = cad.rebuildExact(history, mesh)\nexact.bodies[0].brep        // present: the history has an exact representation\nexact.warnings              // []\ncad.brep.massProperties(exact.bodies[0].brep).volume // 7842.92\u2026 mm\u00b3\ncad.brep.validateBrep(exact.bodies[0].brep).valid    // true"}
    </DocCode>
    <code>rebuild</code> returns <code>bodies</code> (triangle meshes keyed by the feature that created them), the resolved <code>parameters</code>, each sketch's plane, frame, and solve result, the flat patterns of sheet-metal features, and <code>issues</code>. A feature that cannot be built is reported in <code>issues</code> with its feature ID and severity, and it is skipped. Features that do not depend on it still build. <code>rebuildExact(history, given?, options?)</code> can reuse a mesh rebuild you already have, and accepts a <code>budgetMs</code> time budget.
    <p>Base planes are right-handed: <code>XY</code> has u = X, v = Y, normal +Z; <code>YZ</code> has u = Y, v = Z, normal +X; <code>ZX</code> has u = Z, v = X, normal +Y. A sketch, hole, or text can also be placed on the ID of an earlier <code>plane</code> feature, with an <code>offset</code> along the normal.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Key fields and defaults</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>sketch</code></td><td><code>plane</code>, optional <code>offset</code>, <code>sketch</code> (entities, constraints, optional parametric <code>shapes</code>: rectangle, circle, polygon, ellipse, slot, polyline).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>extrude</code></td><td><code>sketch</code>, <code>depth</code>, <code>direction</code> <code>up</code>/<code>down</code>/<code>symmetric</code>, <code>operation</code> <code>new</code>/<code>union</code>/<code>subtract</code>/<code>intersect</code>, optional <code>targets</code> (default: every body so far), <code>edge</code> chamfer/fillet of end faces (<code>ends</code> default <code>both</code>), <code>corner</code> treatment, and <code>taper</code> draft in degrees. Loops inside loops are holes (even-odd).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>revolve</code></td><td><code>sketch</code>, <code>axis</code> (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, or a sketch line), <code>angle</code> in degrees (default 360), <code>operation</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>hole</code></td><td><code>plane</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>at</code> [u, v], <code>diameter</code>, <code>depth</code>; <code>kind</code> <code>simple</code> (default), <code>counterbore</code>, or <code>countersink</code> with <code>headDiameter</code>, <code>headDepth</code>, and <code>angle</code> (default 90°). Optional cosmetic <code>thread</code> note (for example <code>M4</code>), <code>tolerance</code>, and ISO 286 <code>fit</code> such as <code>H7</code>. A cosmetic thread does not change the geometry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pattern</code>, <code>mirror</code></td><td>Copies of an earlier feature's tool, applied the same way.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sweep</code>, <code>loft</code>, <code>coil</code></td><td>Profiles along a path, between sections, or along a helix.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>shell</code>, <code>fillet</code>, <code>draft</code>, <code>pushpull</code></td><td>Face and edge edits.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>move</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>split</code>, <code>combine</code></td><td>Body operations.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rib</code>, <code>sheet</code>, <code>thread</code>, <code>text</code></td><td>Ribs, sheet-metal bodies with flat patterns, modelled threads, and stroke-font text.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plane</code>, <code>primitive</code>, <code>mate</code></td><td>Construction planes, primitive solids, and assembly mating.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Edit the history, not the body">
      <p>Change a parameter and rebuild: every sketch that uses it re-solves and every solid and Boolean after it is redone. Editing a generated mesh directly is lost at the next rebuild.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="brep-primitives" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Exact primitives and placement</h2>
    <p>The B-rep kernel builds analytic solids directly. Each constructor takes an optional placement <code>Frame</code>; without one, the solid is placed in the world frame.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Constructor</th><th>Arguments</th><th>World placement</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>makeBox(dx, dy, dz, placement?)</code></td><td>Edge lengths in mm</td><td>Minimum corner at the origin.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>makeCylinder(radius, height, placement?)</code></td><td>Radius and height</td><td>A cone with equal radii; axis along Z.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>makeCone(r1, r2, height, placement?)</code></td><td>Base and top radii</td><td>Axis along Z.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>makeSphere(radius, placement?)</code></td><td>Radius</td><td>Centred on the origin.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>makeTorus(major, minor, placement?)</code></td><td>Ring and tube radii</td><td>Axis along Z.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocViewer3D title="Exact primitives" caption="makeBox(40, 25, 8) with its corner at the origin, next to a cylinder of radius 6 and height 20 placed beside it. The axes show +X (red), +Y (green), and +Z (blue)." solids={[{"kind":"box","size":[40,25,8]},{"kind":"cylinder","radius":6,"height":20,"at":[60,12.5,0],"color":"#c8a48a"}]} />
    <p>Dimensions must be positive and finite; otherwise the constructors throw a range error. Run <code>validateBrep</code> on anything you build from Booleans, sweeps, or imports before trusting volume or export results. A primitive straight from a constructor is valid by construction.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="cad-best-practices" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Modelling practices that rebuild reliably</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Fix one point and constrain orientation before adding dimensions. An under-constrained sketch can satisfy every dimension and still move at the next rebuild.</li>
      <li>Give every user-facing dimension an <code>id</code> so a conflict report names something a person recognises.</li>
      <li>Prefer parameters and expressions (<code>"width / 2 + 3"</code>, <code>"max(a, b) * 2"</code>) over repeated literals; one edit then drives the whole part.</li>
      <li>Reference earlier features by ID only after they exist in the list. Reordering a history can turn a valid reference into an issue.</li>
      <li>Use <code>targets</code> on Booleans when a part has several bodies. The default applies the operation to every body built so far.</li>
      <li>Check <code>issues</code> after every <code>rebuild</code> and the per-body <code>brep</code> presence after <code>rebuildExact</code> before relying on exact mass properties or STEP export.</li>
      <li>Keep units in millimetres and radians inside sketches. Degree-valued fields are documented as such (revolve angle, taper, countersink angle).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

    <section id="recipes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Design recipes</h2>
    <code>cad.buildRecipe(name, input)</code> turns a few engineering inputs into a full parametric history, plus a one-line summary, the derived numbers and the design rules that produced them. Seven recipes exist; parameters without a default are derived from the others when omitted.
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Title</th><th>Parameters (defaults)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>mounting_plate</code></td><td>Mounting plate</td><td>width=100, depth=60, thickness=10, fastener=M8, fit=medium, counterbore=false, cornerRadius=0, edgeChamfer=0, edgeDistance</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>flange</code></td><td>Bolted flange</td><td>outerDiameter=120, boreDiameter=40, thickness=14, boltCount=6, fastener=M10, fit=medium, boltCircleDiameter, hubDiameter=0, hubHeight=0, edgeChamfer=0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>l_bracket</code></td><td>L bracket</td><td>baseLength=60, legHeight=50, thickness=5, width=40, fastener=M6, fit=medium, holesPerLeg=1, cornerRadius=0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>stepped_shaft</code></td><td>Stepped shaft</td><td>sections, chamfer=1, keywaySection=-1, keyLength</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>pipe_bend</code></td><td>Pipe elbow</td><td>outerDiameter=30, wall=3, legA=100, legB=100, bendAngle=90, bendRadius</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>duct_transition</code></td><td>Duct transition</td><td>shape=rect, width1=80, height1=50, width2=40, height2=40, length=120, wall=2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>enclosure</code></td><td>Enclosure</td><td>length=120, width=80, height=40, wall=3, floor, cornerRadius=6, fastener=M3, bosses=true</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Mounting plate for M8 screws">
{"import { cad } from \u0022@cadcamfun/core\u0022\n\nconst recipe = cad.buildRecipe(\u0022mounting_plate\u0022, { width: 100, depth: 60, thickness: 10, fastener: \u0022M8\u0022 })\nrecipe.summary  // \u0022100 x 60 x 10 mm plate with four holes for M8 screws (\u00d89 mm, medium fit)\u0022\nrecipe.derived  // { holeDiameter: 9, edgeDistance: 18, holeSpacingX: 64, holeSpacingY: 24 }\nrecipe.rules    // [\u0022hole diameter from ISO 273\u0022,\n                //  \u0022centre to edge >= 1.5 x hole diameter\u0022,\n                //  \u0022centre to centre >= 2.5 x hole diameter\u0022]\n\nrecipe.history.parameters  // { width: 100, depth: 60, thick: 10, hole: 9, inset: 18 }\n// features: sketch \u0022outline\u0022, extrude \u0022plate\u0022, holes hole_sw, hole_se, hole_ne, hole_nw\n//           placed at [\u0022inset\u0022,\u0022inset\u0022], [\u0022width - inset\u0022,\u0022inset\u0022], \u2026\n\nconst mesh = cad.rebuild(recipe.history)          // issues: []\nconst exact = cad.rebuildExact(recipe.history, mesh)\ncad.brep.massProperties(exact.bodies[0].brep).volume  // 57455.31 mm\u00b3 = 60000 \u2212 4\u00b7\u03c0\u00b74.5\u00b2\u00b710"}
    </DocCode>
